  "id": "Compound::DB00294 - Gene::5241",
  "verbalization": "Etonogestrel is classified within the Progesterone Congeners group, which includes compounds like Norelgestromin, Norgestimate, and Ethynodiol. These molecules all share a common structural feature of being steroid derivatives, and they bind to the Pgr receptor, a key component of the progestin receptor family. The evidence suggests that Etonogestrel, through its inclusion in the Progesterone Congeners group, interacts with Pgr via a shared mechanism of steroid receptor activation. The lowest common ancestors of Etonogestrel and its congeners are both Progestin and Steroid, indicating a common biological pathway involving steroid hormone receptors. This relationship is supported by the consistent binding of these compounds to Pgr, highlighting a potential role in progestin-related signaling pathways. The integration of these pathways underscores the importance of steroid receptor interactions in the biological activity of Etonogestrel, positioning it within a broader network of progestin-related molecular mechanisms.",
